{"name":"Cold Hearth","hex":"#c1b5a9","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a low temperature.","Causing the air to be cold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A condition of low temperature.","A harsh place; a place of abandonment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cold"},{"term":"hearth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The place in a home where a fire is or was traditionally kept for home heating and for cooking, usually constituted by at least a hearthstone and often enclosed to varying degrees by any combination of reredos, fireplace, oven, smoke hood, or chimney.","A hearthstone, either as standalone or as the floor of an enclosed fireplace or oven."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hearth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cold","blocks":[{"text":"Cold is the presence of low temperature, especially in the atmosphere. In common usage, cold is often a subjective perception. A lower bound to temperature is absolute zero, defined as 0.00 K on the Kelvin scale, an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale. This corresponds to −273.15 °C on the Celsius scale, −459.67 °F on the Fahrenheit scale, and 0.00 °R on the Rankine scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/330px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold","wikidata":"Q270952"},{"title":"Hearth","blocks":[{"text":"A hearth is the place in a home where a fire is or was traditionally kept for home heating and for cooking, usually constituted by a horizontal hearthstone and often enclosed to varying degrees by any combination of reredos, fireplace, oven, smoke hood, or chimney. Hearths are usually composed of masonry such as brick or stone. For millennia, the hearth was such an integral part of a home, usually its central and most important feature, that the concept has been generalized to refer to a homeplace or household, as in the terms \"hearth and home\" and \"keep the home fires burning\". In the modern era, since the advent of central heating, hearths are usually less central to most people's daily life because the heating of the home is instead done by a furnace or a heating stove, and cooking is instead done with a kitchen stove/range alongside other home appliances; thus many homes built in the 20th and 21st centuries do not have hearths. Nonetheless, many homes still have hearths, which still help serve the purposes of warmth, cooking, and comfort."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/RFK-005-Feuerstelle-im-17-Jh.JPG/330px-RFK-005-Feuerstelle-im-17-Jh.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hearth","wikidata":"Q585473"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/500px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/500px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An iceberg, which is commonly associated with cold","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Signal \"cold\" – unofficial (except recommended by CMAS), it is nonetheless used by many schools of diving and propagated through diving websites as one of the more useful additional signals[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiving_signal_cold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Goose bumps, a common physiological response to cold, aiming to reduce the loss of body heat in a cold environment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoose_bumps.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg/500px-AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg/500px-AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A photograph of the snow surface at Dome C Station, Antarctica. A part of the notoriously cold Polar Plateau, it is representative of the majority of the continent's surface.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/L%C3%A9on_Perrault%2C_1890_-_Out_in_the_Cold.jpg/500px-L%C3%A9on_Perrault%2C_1890_-_Out_in_the_Cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/L%C3%A9on_Perrault%2C_1890_-_Out_in_the_Cold.jpg/1280px-L%C3%A9on_Perrault%2C_1890_-_Out_in_the_Cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/L%C3%A9on_Perrault%2C_1890_-_Out_in_the_Cold.jpg/500px-L%C3%A9on_Perrault%2C_1890_-_Out_in_the_Cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm